Falsifies `kernel::arch::x86_64::iommu::proofs::a_remapping_entry_grants_exactly_one_vector_to_exactly_one_requester`.

The defect puts `SVT` two bits low, in `SQ`'s place. The two fields sit side by side in the entry's
high half (`SQ` in 17:16, `SVT` in 19:18), both two bits wide, and `01` is a legal value of each,
so the wrong shift reads as a tidy constant. The result is an entry that verifies nothing: `SVT`
reads 00, so any requester naming the handle passes, and the confinement the whole table exists
for is gone without a fault or a warning.

The boot-time test `a_message_forged_from_another_devices_grant_is_refused` would also go red
under QEMU, which checks `SVT` as the specification does. This harness is the one that says which
field moved.

Expected to fail: the `SQ` assertion, the first check that reads the moved bits (a failed
assertion ends the path, so the `SVT` check after it is never reached). Confirmed red on
2026-11-11 (UTC) on an x86_64 host.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/iommu.rs b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/iommu.rs
index b2a5d41fc..1fb9cbeee 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/iommu.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/iommu.rs
@@ -427,7 +427,7 @@ const ENTRY_VECTOR_SHIFT: u64 = 16;
 const ENTRY_XAPIC_DESTINATION_SHIFT: u64 = 40;
 // High qword: the source id in 15:0, `SQ` in 17:16 (00: compare all sixteen bits) and `SVT` in
 // 19:18 (01: verify the requester id against the source id).
-const ENTRY_VERIFY_REQUESTER_ID: u64 = 0b01 << 18;
+const ENTRY_VERIFY_REQUESTER_ID: u64 = 0b01 << 16;
 
